1
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:05,560
would get up an hour later in the mornings than the other animals, no

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:09,560
complaint was made about that, either.

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:13,159
By the autumn, the animals were tired, but happy.

4
00:00:13,159 --> 00:00:17,720
They had had a hard year, and after the sale of part of the hay and corn, the

5
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:21,760
stores of food for the winter were none too plentiful.

6
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:25,760
But the windmill compensated for everything.

7
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:28,199
It was almost half built now.

8
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:32,600
After the harvest, there was a stretch of clear, dry weather, and the animals

9
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,000
toiled harder than ever, thinking it well worthwhile to plod to and fro all

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,560
day with blocks of stone if, by doing so, they could raise the walls another foot.

11
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:50,039
Boxer would even come out at nights and work for an hour or two on his own by

12
00:00:50,039 --> 00:00:53,000
the light of the harvest moon.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,640
In their spare moments, the animals would walk round and round the

14
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:01,760
half-finished mill, admiring the strength and perpendicularity of its

15
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:08,439
walls, and marveling that they should ever have been able to build anything so imposing.

16
00:01:08,439 --> 00:01:13,920
Only old Benjamin refused to grow enthusiastic about the windmill, though,

17
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:21,920
as usual, he would utter nothing beyond the cryptic remark that "Donkeys live a long time."

18
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,880
November came with raging southwest winds.

19
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:30,520
Building had to stop because it was now too wet to mix the cement.

20
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:35,000
Finally, there came a night when the gale was so violent that the farm

21
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,079
buildings rocked on their foundations, and several tiles were blown off the roof of the barn.

22
00:01:41,079 --> 00:01:45,359
The hens woke up squawking with terror because they had all dreamed

23
00:01:45,359 --> 00:01:50,039
simultaneously of hearing a gun go off in the distance.

24
00:01:50,039 --> 00:01:54,680
In the morning, the animals came out of their stalls to find that the flagstaff

25
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:59,439
had been blown down, and an elm tree at the foot of the orchard had been plucked

26
00:01:59,439 --> 00:02:01,880
up like a radish.

27
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:07,479
They had just noticed this when a cry of despair broke from every animal's throat.

28
00:02:07,479 --> 00:02:10,680
A terrible sight had met their eyes.

29
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:13,720
The windmill was in ruins.

30
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:17,279
With one accord, they dashed down to the spot.

31
00:02:17,279 --> 00:02:22,600
Napoleon, who seldom moved out of a walk, raced ahead of them all.

32
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:28,640
Yes, there it lay, the fruit of all their struggles leveled to its

33
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:36,560
foundations. The stones they had broken and carried so laboriously scattered all around.

34
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:42,000
Unable at first to speak, they stood gazing mournfully at the litter of fallen stone.
